results have previously appeared for various classes of games of take-and-break, for example octal and hexadecimal; see e.g. Winning Ways by Berlekamp, Conway and Guy (1982).\nL18: In this context, our contribution consists of a systematic study of the subclass `break-without-take'.\nL19: Subjects:  | Combinatorics (math.CO); Discrete Mathematics (cs.DM)\nL20: Cite as:  | 【12†arXiv:1803.02621】 [math.CO]\nL21:    | (or 【13†arXiv:1803.02621v3】 [math.CO] for this version)\nL22:    | 【14†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.1803.02621†doi.org】 arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL23: Journal reference:  | Discrete Applied Mathematics, 285 (2020), 509-525\nL24: Related DOI (Digital Object Identifier):  | 【15†https://doi.org/10.1016/j.dam.2020.05.032†doi.org】 DOI(s) linking to related resources\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Antoine Dailly [【16†view email】] [via CCSD proxy]\nL28: 【5†[v1]】 Wed, 7 Mar 2018 12:44:16 UTC (43 KB)\nL29: 【17†[v2]】 Thu, 24 Oct 2019 10:16:43 UTC (47 KB)\nL30: [v3] Fri, 15 May 2020 13:38:00 UTC (47 KB)\nL31: \nL32: Full-text links:\nL33: \nL34: ## Access Paper:\nL35: \nL36: View a PDF of the paper titled Partition games, by Antoine Dailly (UNAM and 4 other authors\nL37: \nL38:   * 【10†View PDF】\nL39:   * 【11†HTML (experimental)】\nL40:   * 【18†TeX Source】\nL41: \nL42: 【19†view license】\nL43: ### Current browse context:\nL44: \nL45: math.CO\nL46: \nL47: 【20†< prev】   |   【21†next >】\nL48: \nL49: 【22†new】 | 【23†recent】 | 【24†2018-03】\nL50: \nL51: Change to browse by:\nL52: \nL53: 【25†cs】\nL54: 【26†cs.DM】\nL55: 【27†math】\nL56: \nL57: ### References & Citations\nL58: \nL59:   * 【28†SciX†scixplorer.org】\nL60:   * 【29†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com】\nL61:   * 【30†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org】\nL62: \nL63: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL64: \nL65: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL66: \nL67: [Button: ×]\nL68: \nL69: loading...\nL70: \nL71: Data provided by:\nL72: \nL73: ### Bookmark\nL74: \nL75: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\n